'connect' at column 8: Expected a subcommand, use 'connmgr [TAB]' for a list of subcommands or enter 'help connmgr'
The Connection manager (CONNMGR) command allows you to import connections from
SQLDeveloper, list and test them.
These connections can be used with the connect command to connect to a database.


Subcommands:

  [1mIMPORT[0m

  Import connection definitions into the common connection store from JSON files
  
  (exported from SQL Developer) or from Oracle Cloud Infrastructure
  DatabaseTools Service.
  - From JSON Files: Connection files exported from SQL Developer may contain
  encrypted passwords and credentials.
    If passwords are encrypted, provide the encryption key (stored as a secret)
  or enter it when prompted. 
    Alternatively, strip passwords during import and provide them later when
  connecting.
  - From OCI DatabaseTools Service: Import a reference to a cloud-based
  connection definition. 
    The connection name is stored locally, but username, connect string, and
  other details are retrieved from the DatabaseTools Service when needed.

  Usage:

    [33mimport[0m {OPTIONS} {PARAMETERS}

  Options:

    [33m-duplicates[0m|[33m-d[0m|[33m-dups[0m <duplicates> (IGNORE)
    How to handle imported connections whose names match an existing connection
    IGNORE: (Default) skip the duplicate connections
    RENAME: rename the duplicate connections by appending a unique number to the
    end of the name
    REPLACE: replace the existing connection with the new connection

    [33m-key[0m <key>
    The public name of a secret that protects the encryption key used to encrypt
    passwords in the file

    [33m-strip-passwords[0m|[33m-s[0m|[33m-strip[0m
    Indicates that any encrypted passwords should be stripped from imported
    connections

    [33m-oci[0m
    Import a connection from the DatabaseTools Service

  Parameters:

    [33m<import-location>[0m [required]
    A single JSON file containing connections exported from SQL Developer, or
    the DisplayName of a DatabaseTools Service connection when used in
    conjunction with the -oci flag

  Examples:

    To import connections from a file, ignoring duplicates and prompting for an
    encryption key for passwords in the file (if needed):
    
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR IMPORT myconns.json[0m
    
    To import connections from a file, renaming duplicates and stripping
    passwords:
    
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR IMPORT -STRIP-PASSWORDS myconns.json[0m
    
    To create a secret for the encryption key and specify the secret name when
    importing connections from a file:
    
      [33mSQL> SECRET SET mySecret <encryption key>[0m
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR IMPORT -KEY mySecret myconns.json[0m


  [1mLIST[0m

  List the existing connection folders and the connections contained within them

  Usage:

    [33mlist[0m {OPTIONS}

  Options:

    [33m-folder[0m <folder>
    Path of the folder to start listing from

    [33m-flat[0m
    List only the name of the connections in a flatten way without the folders
    structure

    [33m-oci[0m
    List connections in the DatabaseTools Service

  Examples:

    To list the currently defined connections in a tree view:
    
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR LIST[0m
    
    To list only the folders and connection under a specific folder (e.g.:
    /prod):
    
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR LIST -folder /prod[0m
    
    To list only the names of the connections without the folders structure:
    
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR LIST -flat[0m
    
    To list all the names of the connections under a folder (e.g.: /dev/) in a
    flatten way:
    
      [33mSQL> CONNMGR LIST -folder /dev/ -flat[0m


